        ],
        "text": "According to Stephen B. Kinnaird, the district court sentenced Marvin Peugh using the 2009 guidelines range of 70 to 87 months instead of the 1998 range of 37 to 46 months that applied at the time of his offense."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Stephen B. Kinnaird",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "9777734eaa3c7da01e7fb7e83448d1f529a421d3e4df9d812fa503cc76067214",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-9777734eaa3c-0000-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Stephen B. Kinnaird, retroactive application of harsher sentencing guidelines may violate the Ex\u202fPost\u202fFacto Clause if it creates a significant risk of increased punishment."
      }
    ],
    "heading": "Overview",
    "status": "complete",
    "summary": "Marvin Peugh challenges the application of the 2009 federal sentencing guidelines, which are harsher than the 1998 guidelines that were in effect when his crime was committed. The issue is whether applying the newer, harsher guidelines retroactively violates the Constitution\u2019s Ex\u202fPost\u202fFacto Clause.",
